    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The following study examined the unique impacts of parental divorce on the college\r\nstudent demographic. Currently enrolled college students were recruited based on whether their\r\nparents had divorced while they were in college and were subsequently interviewed in the\r\nfollowing areas of interest: How the divorce related to their academic motivation, the impact of\r\nthe divorce on the student’s personal well-being, and participant’s attitudes toward romance and\r\nmarriage. College students whose parents either never divorced or divorced during childhood\r\nwere also interviewed as controls.\r\nCollege students were deliberately selected as a population of interest for a few reasons.\r\nFirst, college students whose parents divorce during the college years remain understudied.\r\nFamily literature is rich with the long-term effects of childhood divorce, including adjustment to\r\nand success within higher education, however few studies examine the immediate impact(s) of\r\nparental divorce during a person’s time in college. Second, I hypothesize that this demographic\r\nexperiences parental divorce distinctively, as the college years mark a unique transitional phase\r\nin a young person’s life. Many college students live away from home for the first time, making\r\nthe effects of familial dissolution on those living outside of the household especially interesting.\r\nFinally, college students, unlike young children, are capable of entering into their own romantic\r\nrelationships, with many experimenting with long-term partnership for the first time. Parental\r\ndivorce then may influence college student’s attitudes toward romance and marriage in the short-\r\nterm, which cannot necessarily be said of younger children who have yet to consider or\r\nexperience serious romances first-hand. Emerging out of adolescence and into the early stages of\r\nadulthood, college students thus make up a population in a unique and short-lived phase of\r\ndevelopment whose reactions to parental divorce provide unique insights to family scholars.\r\nResults revealed that parental divorce during the college years creates short-term\r\ndistraction from academic obligations. In low SES demographics, parental divorce is a source of\r\nfinancial stress as parental support for college expenses, including tuition payments, may change\r\ndramatically. Finally, those whose parent’s divorce in college reported skepticism around\r\nmarriage, preferring long-term cohabitation and partnership as an alternative. Other findings\r\noutside of the outlined hypotheses reveal that college students of divorce lose a sense of home as\r\nemphasized by holiday visits, and also may be privy to unwelcomed information about parental conflict given their adult statuses.",
